# Getting Started with Wpromote OpenCode

Welcome to the Wpromote OpenCode configuration.

## Quick Start

1. Install the plugin
2. Restart OpenCode
3. All team configs will be synced automatically

## Configuration

Create `~/.config/opencode/wpromote.json` (or `~/.config/opencode/wpromote.jsonc`) to customize globally:

```json
{
  "$schema": "./schemas/wpromote.schema.json",
  "disable": ["skill:example"],
  "installMethod": "link"
}
```

Add the `$schema` property for autocompletion and inline validation in VS Code, Cursor, and other JSON-aware editors. The plugin automatically copies `wpromote.schema.json` into `~/.config/opencode/schemas/` on startup, so the relative path above works out of the box — no need to clone the source repo or reference a remote URL.

Project-specific settings can live in `.opencode/wpromote.json` or `.opencode/wpromote.jsonc`. Global config loads first, then project config is merged on top, so projects can override team-local preferences without duplicating the whole file. If both `.json` and `.jsonc` exist in the same directory, `.jsonc` wins.

## Optional TUI Sidebar

The package also ships a small OpenCode TUI sidebar that reads the plugin's local status snapshot. Add the package to `~/.config/opencode/cli.json`:

```json
{
  "plugins": ["@wpro-eng/opencode-config/tui"]
}
```

Use the `/tui` subpath for TUI config. The package root is the server plugin for `opencode.json`; the TUI sidebar lives at `@wpro-eng/opencode-config/tui`. Restart OpenCode after changing `cli.json`. The sidebar shows plugin version, loaded config files, asset counts, agent models, and warnings such as local skill conflicts or plugin changes that need a restart.

## Desktop Companion

Wpromote can mirror session activity to a companion state file and launch a bundled companion process. This gives desktop overlays the same basic protocol slim uses: active agents, waiting-for-input state, session cwd, and presentation preferences.

```jsonc
{
  "companion": {
    "enabled": true,
    "binaryPath": "/path/to/wpromote-companion",
    "position": "bottom-right",
    "size": "medium",
    "gifPack": "default",
    "loopStyle": "classic",
    "speed": 1,
    "debug": false
  }
}
```

If `binaryPath` is omitted, Wpromote first checks `$XDG_DATA_HOME/opencode/storage/wpromote-config/bin/wpromote-companion` (or `~/.local/share/opencode/storage/wpromote-config/bin/wpromote-companion`) and then falls back to the companion binary bundled in the npm package. State is written to `opencode-config.companion-state.json`; the bundled companion watches that file, writes `opencode-config.companion-runtime.json` plus a small log in the same storage directory, and opens stacked chromeless macOS desktop image tiles for busy or waiting agents. Idle state stays hidden. Set `binaryPath` only when replacing the bundled companion with a custom one.

Companion agent labels and artwork follow `agentNames`. With `"agentNames": "descriptive"`, active agents use role names such as `orchestrator` and `researcher`; with `"agentNames": "lotr"`, they use `gandalf`, `radagast`, and the rest of the Wpromote LOTR theme. The package includes original SNES-style SVG sprite sheets under `companion/art/descriptive`, `companion/art/lotr`, and `companion/art/common`; each tile bakes the agent name into the art and the bundled macOS overlay cycles through the sprite panels. Restart OpenCode after changing companion config or `agentNames`.

## Runtime Presets

Use presets to switch model and orchestration profiles without rewriting your config by hand:

```jsonc
{
  "preset": "fast",
  "presets": {
    "fast": {
      "description": "Cheaper/faster defaults for routine work",
      "models": {
        "orchestrator": "github-copilot/gpt-5-mini"
      },
      "orchestration": {
        "providerMode": "copilot",
        "limits": {
          "maxConcurrent": 2,
          "maxConcurrentGlobal": 3
        }
      }
    }
  }
}
```

Run `/preset` to list presets, `/preset <name>` to choose one, or `/preset default` to clear the runtime selection. Preset changes apply after restarting OpenCode. `WPROMOTE_PRESET=<name>` overrides both the saved runtime selection and config default for that startup.

## Tmux Multiplexer

Enable Wpromote's tmux runtime under `orchestration.tmux`. The default `mode: "window"` keeps subagents in a dedicated tmux session. Use `mode: "pane"` for oh-my-opencode-slim style pane splitting and layout control:

```jsonc
{
  "orchestration": {
    "tmux": {
      "enabled": true,
      "mode": "pane",
      "layout": "main-vertical",
      "mainPaneSize": 60
    }
  }
}
```

`mainPaneSize` is the Wpromote equivalent of slim's `main_pane_size`. Supported layouts are `main-horizontal`, `main-vertical`, `tiled`, `even-horizontal`, and `even-vertical`.

## Per-Agent Policy Controls

Use `agentOverrides.<agent>.policy` to narrow a team agent's tools, MCP-derived tool permissions, or skills without replacing the whole agent. Built-in team agents accept either descriptive or LOTR names:

```jsonc
{
  "agentOverrides": {
    "researcher": {
      "policy": {
        "tools": { "bash": false },
        "permission": { "edit": "deny" },
        "mcps": ["context7", "exa", "!chrome-devtools"],
        "skills": ["*", "!terraform"]
      }
    }
  }
}
```

`mcps` and `skills` use allow entries by default and deny entries when prefixed with `!`. A list with positive entries becomes an allowlist for known team assets; a deny-only list becomes a blocklist. These controls are applied to the bundled team agent before OpenCode sees it. A local `opencode.json` agent with the same name still wins completely.

## Overriding Team Configs

To override a team skill/agent/command, create a local file with the same name in your personal config directory.

If you use `installMethod: "copy"` and edit a copied team skill under `_plugins/opencode-config`, startup will preserve your edited copy and stage the package update under `_plugins/opencode-config/.updates/` for manual review instead of overwriting it.

# Wpromote Orchestration Runtime

Use the wpromote orchestration control surface for long-running and delegated work.

## Core Controls

- `/continue` enables continuous loop mode for the session.
- `/stop` marks active work in the current parent session stopped, then waits for further instruction.
- `/tasks` shows active/recent subagent tasks.
- `/task <id>` shows one task record.
- `/diagnostics` runs full orchestration diagnostics.

## Delegation Discipline

When using delegated execution:

1. Provide a short, specific task title.
2. Include expected outcome and verification criteria.
3. Start with at most one child. Add parallel children only for independent work when active capacity remains.
4. Keep updates concise and state-driven: started, completed, failed, interrupted, stopped.
5. Monitor active and recent task health during long-running work.
6. Cancel disposable background work once its value is exhausted.

Raw subagent calls are admitted before launch under a local filesystem lock. Defaults are 3 active tasks per session and 5 active tasks globally across local OpenCode processes. Optional `limits.providerMaxConcurrent.copilot` and `.native` values can lower provider-specific capacity. Rejections are final for that call and create no queued task record.

Foreground capacity is released on terminal subagent tool updates. Background launches retain capacity until a matching synthetic child result, child execution failure or interruption, or child session deletion arrives. Idle signals are observational and do not release capacity; duplicate terminal signals are ignored.

## Provider Routing

- Default provider mode is `copilot`.
- 408, 429, 503, and 504 failures configured in `runtimeFallback.retryOnErrors` reduce shared provider capacity and establish the configured cooldown. Successful completions recover capacity additively.
- If service quality or budget requires it, switch to `native` in `wpromote.json`:

```json
{
  "orchestration": {
    "providerMode": "native",
    "categories": {
      "routing": {
        "quick": "native"
      },
      "maxConcurrent": {
        "quick": 1
      }
    }
  }
}
```

## Recovery

- Startup recovery is enabled by default (`orchestration.recovery.autoResumeOnStart = true`).
- Running task records owned by a live process remain active across plugin instances.
- Abandoned running records and legacy queued/retrying records become terminal `interrupted` records. The plugin cannot safely replay an original task call, so it does not create a dispatch queue.

```json
{
  "orchestration": {
    "recovery": {
      "autoResumeOnStart": true
    }
  }
}
```

## Tmux Runtime

- Enable tmux session management with `orchestration.tmux.enabled`.
- The default `mode: "window"` creates a dedicated tmux session (`{sessionPrefix}-{opencode-session-id}`) for each OpenCode session.
- Each subagent task gets its own window inside that session, keeping your working window clean.
- Use `tmux switch-client -t wpo-*` to peek at agent work.
- Use `mode: "pane"` for oh-my-opencode-slim style behavior: each task opens as a pane in the current tmux window, then Wpromote applies `layout` and `mainPaneSize`.

```json
{
  "orchestration": {
    "tmux": {
      "enabled": true,
      "mode": "window",
      "sessionPrefix": "wpo"
    }
  }
}
```

Slim-style pane layout parity:

```json
{
  "orchestration": {
    "tmux": {
      "enabled": true,
      "mode": "pane",
      "layout": "main-vertical",
      "mainPaneSize": 60
    }
  }
}
```

- Supported layouts are `main-horizontal`, `main-vertical`, `tiled`, `even-horizontal`, and `even-vertical`.
- `/tasks` reports the tmux mode, layout, main pane size, agent session name, and active window/pane count.
- `/diagnostics` checks tmux wiring, binary availability, and window reconciliation.

